speechtext:
It is true the Gill report was an excuse for inaction, and that action was delayed far too long. We are now trying to cope with the Gill report and to bring in recommendations which are very sweeping in character and which can be implemented. There is no question in my mind, as a result of reading the Gill report, that a revision of the unemployment insurance fund is long overdue. Certainly it cannot be left just hanging in the air, particularly when the fund itself is so perilously close to being insolvent and is living from hand to mouth far too frequently.
I commend the hon. member for calling attention to the problem. It is perfectly legitimate on his part to use the device of drafting a piece of legislation, however imperfect, and dealing generally with the problem, as a means of calling attention to it. But we have to be responsible in this house too, and it is much too easy to say, "Let us do this;

let us give this benefit." In the case of the unemployment insurance fund we could not have a clearer warning that we cannot proceed in a piecemeal way without making the necessary fiscal recommendations for the solvency of the fund itself, to make it as solvent as it used to be at one time, to make it the kind of fund which serves the purpose of insurance and does not pervert the concept of social insurance to a form of welfare measure, which perhaps might be more appropriately administered under one of the other social welfare programs being developed in Canada. I for one cannot accept the bill before us. While the intention is perfectly legitimate and I have the deepest sympathy for it, I do not think that clause 3 of the bill achieves the purpose which the hon. member has in mind and in my view it negates to a substantial degree what he has tried to do.
